This study conducts a comprehensive analysis of the spectral and orbital stability of soliton solutions for the one-dimensional $\U(1)$-invariant nonlinear Klein--Gordon equation, which corresponds to the anharmonic oscillator with a $\lambda\varphi^{4}+\mu\varphi^{2}$ potential. The investigation begins with a detailed examination of the operator governing linearized stability, achieved through the construction of an explicit parametric form for its eigenfunctions. Leveraging this framework, the authors establish a complete description of the eigenvalue spectrum and derive a full characterization of soliton orbital stability. Finally, these theoretical findings are validated numerically by exploring spectrum regions beyond the scope of the explicit derivation. The research confirms that no stable orbital soliton solutions exist when the potential is a double-well type ($\lambda>0$ and $\mu<0$). Furthermore, for the single-well case ($\lambda>0$ and $\mu>0$), soliton spectral and orbital stabilities are equivalent, aligning with the principle that for systems with a finite-dimensional instability along the ground state, spectral and orbital (and consequently, linear and nonlinear) instabilities are congruent. This work extends the results presented in \cite{DelourBea19} regarding the $\lambda\varphi^{2}$ anharmonic oscillator potential.  // This research was supported by the Natural Science Foundation of China (Grant No. 11971233).  // The authors express gratitude to the anonymous referees for their constructive comments, which significantly enhanced the quality of this manuscript.  // Y. Delourme acknowledges support from the F.R.S.-FNRS under grant no. T.0117.19.  // Y. Delourme, B. Beauchard: On spectral and orbital stability for the
